# IP addresses and prefixes as values, the way Go's `net/netip` holds
# them and Python's `ipaddress` reads and classifies them: no socket, no
# resolver, nothing of the platform. `std/socket` connects to addresses;
# this module is for what a program decides about one - is it loopback,
# is it inside 10.0.0.0/8, which route of a table is the longest that
# holds it - and it can be imported by code that must not reach the
# network or the clock.
#
# `IP` is either family in one value: 32 or 128 bits, and for IPv6 a zone
# (`fe80::1%eth0`, RFC 4007 section 11), which is kept, printed, and part
# of `==` and of the order, as Go and Python keep it. An IPv4-mapped IPv6
# address (`::ffff:192.0.2.1`, RFC 4291 section 2.5.5.2) is an IPv6
# address, not equal to the IPv4 one, as in both of those; `unmap` and
# `ipv4_mapped` take it apart and `ipv6_mapped` makes one.
#
# Reading is Python 3.14's `ip_address`, rule for rule, because Python's
# rules are the strict ones and a program checking an allowlist must not
# read an address another reader reads differently. IPv4 is four decimal
# octets of one to three ASCII digits, none past 255 and none with a
# leading zero: `010.0.0.1` is refused, because glibc's `inet_aton` reads
# `010` as octal 8 (CVE-2021-29921, Python's bpo-36384), and so are the
# shorthand forms `inet_aton` takes (`127.1`, `0x7f.1`). IPv6 is RFC 4291
# section 2.2's text: eight groups of one to four hex digits, one `::` for
# a run of zero groups, an IPv4 dotted tail in place of the last two, at
# most 45 characters before the zone, and a zone that is not empty and
# holds no second `%`. A `/` anywhere is refused: that is a prefix.
#
# Writing is RFC 5952 section 4: lower case, no leading zeros, the
# longest run of two or more zero groups as `::` (the first of two equal
# runs), a single zero group written `0`, and an IPv4-mapped address with
# its dotted tail - what Python 3.14's `str` writes. `exploded` is every
# group in four digits, and `reverse_pointer` the `in-addr.arpa` or
# `ip6.arpa` name (RFC 1035 section 3.5, RFC 3596 section 2.5). For a
# zoned address `exploded` keeps the zone, as Python 3.14.8 does, and
# `reverse_pointer` leaves it out, a PTR name having no place for one;
# 3.14.8 writes the zone reversed into the name. Python before 3.14.8
# raised on both.
#
# Order is Go's `Addr.Compare`: IPv4 before IPv6, then by value, then by
# zone; what Python's `get_mixed_type_key` sorts by, the zone added.
# `next` and `prev` are the neighbouring address, nil past either end of
# the family (Python raises there), the zone kept.
#
# Classification follows Python 3.14, whose `is_global` reads the IANA
# IPv4 and IPv6 Special-Purpose Address Registries (RFC 6890 and its
# updates): `global?` is false for every block the registry marks not
# globally reachable, the exceptions inside them (192.0.0.9, 2001:3::/32,
# and the rest) being global again, and for 100.64.0.0/10 (RFC 6598's
# shared space, neither global nor private by Python's reading).
# `loopback?`, `link_local?`, `multicast?`, `unspecified?`, `reserved?`
# and `site_local?` are Python's predicates of those names. `private?` is
# not Python's `is_private`, which means "not globally reachable": it is
# Go's `IsPrivate`, RFC 1918's three IPv4 blocks and RFC 4193's fc00::/7,
# the question "is this one of the networks people number their own
# machines from". Every predicate of an IPv4-mapped address is the
# predicate of the IPv4 address inside it, as Python 3.13 made it - but
# `site_local?`, which Python reads off the IPv6 bits alone.
#
# `Prefix` is an address and a length - a CIDR block, RFC 4632. `parse`
# reads what Python's `ip_network` reads: an address, then `/` and a
# decimal length (leading zeros allowed, as Python allows them), or for
# IPv4 a dotted netmask (`/255.255.255.0`) or hostmask (`/0.0.0.255`),
# all ones and all zeros read as netmasks; with no `/`, the address
# alone, at full length. Host bits set past the length are refused, as
# Python's `strict=True` refuses them, unless `mask: true` asks for them
# cleared. A zone is refused in a prefix, as Go refuses it, where Python
# keeps it. `Prefix.new(ip, length)` keeps the host bits it is given, as
# Go's `PrefixFrom` does, to hold an interface's address with its
# network's length; `masked` clears them, and everything that answers
# about the block - `first`, `last`, `contains?`, `overlaps?`, subnets,
# supernets - answers about the masked one. Containment never crosses
# families (an IPv4 address is in no IPv6 prefix, a mapped address in no
# IPv4 one) and ignores a zone, as Python's `in` does. `subnets(m)`
# answers at most 65,536 prefixes at once; `subnet(m, i)` reaches any one.
#
# `PrefixSet` holds prefixes of both families in a binary trie, one node
# a bit: `lookup` answers the longest prefix holding an address, the
# routing table's question, in at most 33 or 129 steps however many
# prefixes it holds; `contains?` is an allowlist's question. Neither
# allocates, nor do `Prefix#contains?` and the predicates: 40,000 of
# those calls together allocated 0 bytes, plain and --release, measured
# by the exercise. `collapsed` answers the fewest prefixes covering
# exactly the same addresses, what Python's `collapse_addresses` answers
# per family.
#
# What is wrong with text is a `NetipError` value naming the text and
# why. What panics is a program's own mistake: a length outside 0 to 32
# or 0 to 128, a zone given to an IPv4 address, a zone with a `%`, a byte
# count that is neither 4 nor 16, a subnet length shorter than the prefix
# or a supernet length longer.

module std/netip

import std/int
import std/traits::{Comparable}

# Why a text is not an address or a prefix: `message` names the text.
pub struct NetipError
  getter message : String

  def initialize(@message : String)
  end

  def to_s : String
    @message
  end
end

impl Error for NetipError
  def message : String
    @message
  end
end

# An IPv4 or IPv6 address, an IPv6 one with its zone ("" for none).
pub struct IP
  # Two words, not one `UInt128`: a struct holding a `UInt128` wants 16-byte
  # alignment, which an array's buffer past a few pages does not get, and
  # an optimised build then reads it with an aligned load and faults -
  # 65,536 subnets in an array, read back, ended in SIGSEGV under --release.
  @high : UInt64
  @low : UInt64
  @v6 : ::Bool
  @zone : String

  protected def initialize(*, value : UInt128, v6 : ::Bool, zone : String)
    @high = (value >> 64).to_u64
    @low = (value & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF_u128).to_u64
    @v6 = v6
    @zone = zone
  end

  # The IPv4 address *a*.*b*.*c*.*d*.
  def self.v4(a : UInt8, b : UInt8, c : UInt8, d : UInt8) : IP
    new(value: (a.to_u128 << 24) | (b.to_u128 << 16) | (c.to_u128 << 8) | d.to_u128, v6: false, zone: "")
  end

  # The IPv4 address whose 32 bits, network order, are *value*.
  def self.v4(value : UInt32) : IP
    new(value: value.to_u128, v6: false, zone: "")
  end

  # The IPv6 address whose 128 bits, network order, are *value*.
  def self.v6(value : UInt128) : IP
    new(value: value, v6: true, zone: "")
  end

  # Four bytes are an IPv4 address and sixteen an IPv6 one, network order,
  # as `bytes` answers them.
  def self.from_bytes(bytes : Array(UInt8)) : IP
    raise "IP: #{bytes.size} bytes are no address; IPv4 is 4 and IPv6 is 16" unless bytes.size == 4 || bytes.size == 16
    value = 0_u128
    bytes.each { |byte| value = (value << 8) | byte.to_u128 }
    new(value: value, v6: bytes.size == 16, zone: "")
  end

  # Either family, as Python's `ip_address` reads it: IPv4 when it is
  # IPv4, else IPv6. Text with a colon can only be IPv6 and text without
  # one only IPv4, so the refusal is the one family's that applies.
  def self.parse(text : String) : IP | NetipError
    return parse_v6(text) if NetipBits.find(text, 58_u8, 0) >= 0
    parse_v4(text)
  end

  # Dotted decimal, four octets, Python's `IPv4Address`.
  def self.parse_v4(text : String) : IP | NetipError
    return NetipError.new("netip: an empty string is not an address") if text.empty?
    if NetipBits.find(text, 47_u8, 0) >= 0
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not an address: it holds a '/', which a prefix has and Prefix.parse reads")
    end
    read = NetipBits.read_v4(text)
    return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not an IPv4 address: #{read}") if read.is_a?(String)
    new(value: read, v6: false, zone: "")
  end

  # RFC 4291 text with an optional `%zone`, Python's `IPv6Address`.
  def self.parse_v6(text : String) : IP | NetipError
    return NetipError.new("netip: an empty string is not an address") if text.empty?
    if NetipBits.find(text, 47_u8, 0) >= 0
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not an address: it holds a '/', which a prefix has and Prefix.parse reads")
    end
    address = text
    zone = ""
    percent = NetipBits.find(text, 37_u8, 0)
    if percent >= 0
      address = text[0, percent]
      zone = text[percent + 1, text.bytesize - percent - 1]
      why = NetipBits.zone_refused(zone)
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not an IPv6 address: #{why}") if why
    end
    read = NetipBits.read_v6(address)
    return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not an IPv6 address: #{read}") if read.is_a?(String)
    new(value: read, v6: true, zone: zone)
  end

  def v4? : ::Bool
    !@v6
  end

  def v6? : ::Bool
    @v6
  end

  # 32 or 128.
  def bit_length : Int32
    @v6 ? 128 : 32
  end

  # The address's bits, network order, in the low 32 for IPv4.
  def to_u128 : UInt128
    (@high.to_u128 << 64) | @low.to_u128
  end

  # The zone, or nil for none (and always for IPv4).
  def zone : String?
    @zone.empty? ? nil : @zone
  end

  # This address in *zone*, or with none for `""`. Python's rules for a
  # zone hold: no `%` in it. IPv4 has no zones.
  def with_zone(zone : String) : IP
    raise "IP: #{to_s} is IPv4, which has no zones" unless @v6 || zone.empty?
    why = zone.empty? ? nil : NetipBits.zone_refused(zone)
    raise "IP: #{why}" if why
    IP.new(value: to_u128, v6: @v6, zone: zone)
  end

  # Four or sixteen bytes, network order.
  def bytes : Array(UInt8)
    count = @v6 ? 16 : 4
    result = Array(UInt8).new(count)
    index = count - 1
    while index >= 0
      result << (to_u128 >> (index * 8)).unsafe_to_u8
      index = index - 1
    end
    result
  end

  # The IPv4 address inside an IPv4-mapped one (`::ffff:a.b.c.d`), or nil.
  def ipv4_mapped : IP?
    return nil unless @v6 && (to_u128 >> 32) == 0xFFFF_u128
    IP.new(value: to_u128 & 0xFFFFFFFF_u128, v6: false, zone: "")
  end

  # The IPv4 address inside an IPv4-mapped one, any other address itself.
  def unmap : IP
    ipv4_mapped || self
  end

  # `::ffff:a.b.c.d` for an IPv4 address (RFC 4291 section 2.5.5.2).
  def ipv6_mapped : IP
    raise "IP: #{to_s} is IPv6 already; ipv6_mapped maps IPv4" if @v6
    IP.new(value: to_u128 | (0xFFFF_u128 << 32), v6: true, zone: "")
  end

  # The address after this one, nil after the family's last.
  def next : IP?
    return nil if to_u128 == max_value
    IP.new(value: to_u128 &+ 1_u128, v6: @v6, zone: @zone)
  end

  # The address before this one, nil before the family's first.
  def prev : IP?
    return nil if to_u128 == 0_u128
    IP.new(value: to_u128 &- 1_u128, v6: @v6, zone: @zone)
  end

  # The prefix of *length* bits holding this address, its host bits
  # cleared and its zone left behind.
  def prefix(length : Int32) : Prefix
    NetipBits.check_length(self, length)
    Prefix.new(IP.new(value: to_u128 & NetipBits.netmask(@v6, length), v6: @v6, zone: ""), length)
  end

  protected def max_value : UInt128
    @v6 ? ~0_u128 : 0xFFFFFFFF_u128
  end

  # --- classification ---

  # 127.0.0.0/8, ::1.
  def loopback? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return NetipBits.within(v4, 0x7F000000_u128, 8, 32)
    end
    to_u128 == 1_u128
  end

  # 0.0.0.0, ::.
  def unspecified? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return v4 == 0_u128
    end
    to_u128 == 0_u128
  end

  # 169.254.0.0/16 (RFC 3927), fe80::/10.
  def link_local? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return NetipBits.within(v4, 0xA9FE0000_u128, 16, 32)
    end
    NetipBits.within(to_u128, 0xFE80_u128 << 112, 10, 128)
  end

  # 224.0.0.0/4, ff00::/8.
  def multicast? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return NetipBits.within(v4, 0xE0000000_u128, 4, 32)
    end
    NetipBits.within(to_u128, 0xFF_u128 << 120, 8, 128)
  end

  # 240.0.0.0/4 (RFC 1112's class E), and for IPv6 the blocks RFC 4291
  # section 2.4 leaves unassigned: Python's `is_reserved`.
  def reserved? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return NetipBits.within(v4, 0xF0000000_u128, 4, 32)
    end
    NetipBits.listed(to_u128, NetipBits::RESERVED_V6, 128)
  end

  # fec0::/10, deprecated by RFC 3879; read off the IPv6 bits alone, as
  # Python reads it, and never IPv4.
  def site_local? : ::Bool
    @v6 && NetipBits.within(to_u128, 0xFEC0_u128 << 112, 10, 128)
  end

  # RFC 1918 (10/8, 172.16/12, 192.168/16) and RFC 4193 (fc00::/7): the
  # networks people number their own machines from, Go's `IsPrivate`.
  def private? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return NetipBits.within(v4, 0x0A000000_u128, 8, 32) || NetipBits.within(v4, 0xAC100000_u128, 12, 32) || NetipBits.within(v4, 0xC0A80000_u128, 16, 32)
    end
    NetipBits.within(to_u128, 0xFC_u128 << 120, 7, 128)
  end

  # Globally reachable by the IANA special-purpose registries, as Python
  # 3.14's `is_global` reads them.
  def global? : ::Bool
    if v4 = v4_view
      return false if NetipBits.within(v4, 0x64400000_u128, 10, 32)
      return !NetipBits.listed(v4, NetipBits::NOT_GLOBAL_V4, 32) || NetipBits.listed(v4, NetipBits::GLOBAL_V4, 32)
    end
    !NetipBits.listed(to_u128, NetipBits::NOT_GLOBAL_V6, 128) || NetipBits.listed(to_u128, NetipBits::GLOBAL_V6, 128)
  end

  # The 32 bits a predicate reads for IPv4: the address's own, or the
  # ones inside an IPv4-mapped address; nil for any other IPv6.
  private def v4_view : UInt128?
    return to_u128 unless @v6
    return to_u128 & 0xFFFFFFFF_u128 if (to_u128 >> 32) == 0xFFFF_u128
    nil
  end

  # --- text ---

  def to_s : String
    return NetipBits.dotted(to_u128) unless @v6
    text = if (to_u128 >> 32) == 0xFFFF_u128
             "::ffff:" + NetipBits.dotted(to_u128 & 0xFFFFFFFF_u128)
           else
             NetipBits.hextets(to_u128)
           end
    @zone.empty? ? text : text + "%" + @zone
  end

  def to_s(io : IO) : Nil
    io.write(to_s)
  end

  def inspect : String
    to_s
  end

  # Every IPv6 group in four digits (an IPv4-mapped tail kept dotted, as
  # Python writes it); IPv4 as `to_s` writes it.
  def exploded : String
    return NetipBits.dotted(to_u128) unless @v6
    text = String.build do |io|
      index = 0
      while index < 8
        io << ':' if index > 0
        io << NetipBits.hextet(to_u128, index).to_s(16).rjust(4, '0')
        index = index + 1
      end
    end
    text = text[0, 30] + NetipBits.dotted(to_u128 & 0xFFFFFFFF_u128) if (to_u128 >> 32) == 0xFFFF_u128
    @zone.empty? ? text : text + "%" + @zone
  end

  # The name a PTR record for this address is under: `4.3.2.1.in-addr.arpa`,
  # or thirty-two nibbles and `ip6.arpa`.
  def reverse_pointer : String
    String.build do |io|
      if @v6
        index = 0
        while index < 32
          io << ((to_u128 >> (index * 4)) & 15_u128).to_i32.to_s(16)
          io << '.'
          index = index + 1
        end
        io << "ip6.arpa"
      else
        index = 0
        while index < 4
          io << ((to_u128 >> (index * 8)) & 255_u128).to_i32
          io << '.'
          index = index + 1
        end
        io << "in-addr.arpa"
      end
    end
  end

  # --- comparison ---

  # IPv4 first, then by value, then by zone (none first).
  def <=>(other : IP) : Int32
    return @v6 ? 1 : -1 if @v6 != other.v6?
    return to_u128 < other.to_u128 ? -1 : 1 if to_u128 != other.to_u128
    mine = @zone
    theirs = other.zone || ""
    return 0 if mine == theirs
    mine < theirs ? -1 : 1
  end

  def ==(other : IP) : ::Bool
    @v6 == other.v6? && to_u128 == other.to_u128 && @zone == (other.zone || "")
  end

  def hash : Int32
    mix = to_u128.hash
    mix = (mix &* 31) &+ (@v6 ? 1 : 0)
    (mix &* 31) &+ @zone.hash
  end
end

impl Comparable for IP
  def <=>(other : self) : Int32
    self <=> other
  end
end

# An address and a length: a CIDR block (RFC 4632), with the address's
# host bits as it was given them.
pub struct Prefix
  getter ip : IP
  getter length : Int32

  # *ip* and *length* as they are, host bits and all; `IP#prefix` clears
  # them. A zoned address is refused: a block has no zone.
  def initialize(@ip : IP, @length : Int32)
    NetipBits.check_length(@ip, @length)
    raise "Prefix: #{@ip} has a zone, and a prefix has none" if @ip.zone
  end

  # Python's `ip_network(text, strict: !mask)`, a zone refused.
  def self.parse(text : String, mask : ::Bool = false) : Prefix | NetipError
    slash = NetipBits.find(text, 47_u8, 0)
    address = slash < 0 ? text : text[0, slash]
    tail = slash < 0 ? nil : text[slash + 1, text.bytesize - slash - 1]
    if slash >= 0 && NetipBits.find(text, 47_u8, slash + 1) >= 0
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not a prefix: it has more than one '/'")
    end
    ip = IP.parse(address)
    return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not a prefix: #{NetipBits.reason(ip.message)}") if ip.is_a?(NetipError)
    if ip.zone
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not a prefix: the address has a zone, and a prefix has none")
    end
    length = tail ? NetipBits.read_length(tail, ip.v6?) : ip.bit_length
    if length < 0
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not a prefix: #{tail.inspect} is not a length from 0 to #{ip.bit_length}#{ip.v6? ? "" : ", a netmask or a hostmask"}")
    end
    clear = ip.to_u128 & NetipBits.netmask(ip.v6?, length)
    if clear != ip.to_u128
      return NetipError.new("netip: #{text.inspect} is not a prefix: host bits are set past /#{length}; Prefix.parse(text, mask: true) clears them") unless mask
      return ip.prefix(length)
    end
    Prefix.new(ip, length)
  end

  def v4? : ::Bool
    @ip.v4?
  end

  def v6? : ::Bool
    @ip.v6?
  end

  # 32 or 128.
  def bit_length : Int32
    @ip.bit_length
  end

  # This prefix with the address's host bits cleared.
  def masked : Prefix
    @ip.prefix(@length)
  end

  # The block's first address (Python's `network_address`).
  def first : IP
    NetipBits.ip(@ip.v6?, @ip.to_u128 & NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length))
  end

  # The block's last address (Python's `broadcast_address`).
  def last : IP
    mask = NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length)
    NetipBits.ip(@ip.v6?, (@ip.to_u128 & mask) | (NetipBits.all_ones(@ip.v6?) & ~mask))
  end

  # The length as an address: `255.255.255.0` for a /24.
  def netmask : IP
    NetipBits.ip(@ip.v6?, NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length))
  end

  # The host bits as an address: `0.0.0.255` for a /24.
  def hostmask : IP
    NetipBits.ip(@ip.v6?, NetipBits.all_ones(@ip.v6?) & ~NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length))
  end

  # Whether *ip* is in the block: never across families, the zone not read.
  def contains?(ip : IP) : ::Bool
    return false if ip.v6? != @ip.v6?
    mask = NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length)
    (ip.to_u128 & mask) == (@ip.to_u128 & mask)
  end

  # Whether all of *other* is in this block (Python's `supernet_of`).
  def contains?(other : Prefix) : ::Bool
    other.v6? == @ip.v6? && other.length >= @length && contains?(other.ip)
  end

  # Whether every address of this block is in *other*.
  def subnet_of?(other : Prefix) : ::Bool
    other.contains?(self)
  end

  # Whether the two blocks share an address: as blocks nest or are
  # apart, one holds the other's first address or neither shares any.
  def overlaps?(other : Prefix) : ::Bool
    other.v6? == @ip.v6? && (contains?(other.ip) || other.contains?(@ip))
  end

  # The block one bit shorter; a /0 is its own.
  def supernet : Prefix
    @length == 0 ? masked : supernet(@length - 1)
  end

  # The block of *new_length* holding this one.
  def supernet(new_length : Int32) : Prefix
    raise "Prefix: a supernet of #{self} is shorter, and /#{new_length} is not" if new_length > @length || new_length < 0
    @ip.prefix(new_length)
  end

  # How many /*new_length* blocks this one splits into, at most 65,536
  # answered at once.
  MAX_SUBNETS = 65536

  # The /*new_length* blocks this one splits into, in order.
  def subnets(new_length : Int32) : Array(Prefix)
    check_subnet_length(new_length)
    if new_length - @length > 16
      raise "Prefix: #{self} into /#{new_length} is 2^#{new_length - @length} subnets, past the #{MAX_SUBNETS} subnets answers at once; subnet(new_length, index) reaches any one"
    end
    count = 1 << (new_length - @length)
    result = Array(Prefix).new(count)
    index = 0
    while index < count
      result << subnet(new_length, index.to_u128)
      index = index + 1
    end
    result
  end

  # The *index*th /*new_length* block of this one, from 0.
  def subnet(new_length : Int32, index : UInt128) : Prefix
    check_subnet_length(new_length)
    room = new_length - @length
    if room < 128 && index >= (1_u128 << room)
      raise "Prefix: #{self} has 2^#{room} subnets of /#{new_length}, and #{index} is past them"
    end
    base = @ip.to_u128 & NetipBits.netmask(@ip.v6?, @length)
    Prefix.new(NetipBits.ip(@ip.v6?, base | (index << (bit_length - new_length))), new_length)
  end

  private def check_subnet_length(new_length : Int32) : Nil
    if new_length < @length || new_length > bit_length
      raise "Prefix: a subnet of #{self} is /#{@length} to /#{bit_length}, and /#{new_length} is not"
    end
  end

  def to_s : String
    "#{@ip}/#{@length}"
  end

  def to_s(io : IO) : Nil
    io.write(to_s)
  end

  def inspect : String
    to_s
  end

  # IPv4 first, then by first address, then shorter first, then by the
  # address as held - Python's order of networks, host bits after.
  def <=>(other : Prefix) : Int32
    return v6? ? 1 : -1 if v6? != other.v6?
    mine = first.to_u128
    theirs = other.first.to_u128
    return mine < theirs ? -1 : 1 if mine != theirs
    return @length < other.length ? -1 : 1 if @length != other.length
    @ip <=> other.ip
  end

  def ==(other : Prefix) : ::Bool
    @length == other.length && @ip == other.ip
  end

  def hash : Int32
    (@ip.hash &* 31) &+ @length
  end
end

impl Comparable for Prefix
  def <=>(other : self) : Int32
    self <=> other
  end
end

# Prefixes of both families in a binary trie, one node a bit: node 0 is
# the IPv4 root and node 1 the IPv6 one, and a node's children are made
# after it, so each has a larger index than its parent - which is what
# lets `collapsed` decide a node from its children in one backward pass.
# A removed prefix leaves its nodes behind, unmarked.
pub class PrefixSet
  @zero : Array(Int32)
  @one : Array(Int32)
  @member : Array(::Bool)
  @size : Int32

  def initialize
    @zero = [-1, -1]
    @one = [-1, -1]
    @member = [false, false]
    @size = 0
  end

  def initialize(prefixes : Array(Prefix))
    @zero = [-1, -1]
    @one = [-1, -1]
    @member = [false, false]
    @size = 0
    prefixes.each { |prefix| add(prefix) }
  end

  # How many prefixes the set holds.
  def size : Int32
    @size
  end

  def empty? : ::Bool
    @size == 0
  end

  # Adds *prefix*, masked; false when it was there.
  def add(prefix : Prefix) : ::Bool
    node = prefix.v6? ? 1 : 0
    value = prefix.ip.to_u128
    top = prefix.bit_length - 1
    depth = 0
    while depth < prefix.length
      bit = (value >> (top - depth)) & 1_u128
      child = bit == 0_u128 ? @zero[node] : @one[node]
      if child < 0
        child = @member.size
        @zero << -1
        @one << -1
        @member << false
        if bit == 0_u128
          @zero[node] = child
        else
          @one[node] = child
        end
      end
      node = child
      depth = depth + 1
    end
    return false if @member[node]
    @member[node] = true
    @size = @size + 1
    true
  end

  # Removes *prefix*, masked; false when it was not there.
  def delete(prefix : Prefix) : ::Bool
    node = find(prefix)
    return false if node < 0 || !@member[node]
    @member[node] = false
    @size = @size - 1
    true
  end

  # Whether *prefix*, masked, is one of the set's - this prefix, not one
  # holding it; `lookup(prefix.first)` asks that.
  def includes?(prefix : Prefix) : ::Bool
    node = find(prefix)
    node >= 0 && @member[node]
  end

  # Whether some prefix of the set holds *ip*.
  def contains?(ip : IP) : ::Bool
    deepest(ip) >= 0
  end

  # The longest prefix of the set holding *ip*, or nil.
  def lookup(ip : IP) : Prefix?
    depth = deepest(ip)
    return nil if depth < 0
    ip.prefix(depth)
  end

  # Every prefix of the set, IPv4 first, in `Prefix` order.
  def prefixes : Array(Prefix)
    result = [] of Prefix
    collect(0, 0, 0_u128, false, result, nil)
    collect(1, 0, 0_u128, true, result, nil)
    result
  end

  # The fewest prefixes holding exactly the addresses the set's do,
  # IPv4 first, in order: Python's `collapse_addresses`, per family.
  def collapsed : Array(Prefix)
    full = Array(::Bool).new(@member.size, false)
    node = @member.size - 1
    while node >= 0
      zero = @zero[node]
      one = @one[node]
      full[node] = @member[node] || (zero >= 0 && one >= 0 && full[zero] && full[one])
      node = node - 1
    end
    result = [] of Prefix
    collect(0, 0, 0_u128, false, result, full)
    collect(1, 0, 0_u128, true, result, full)
    result
  end

  # The node of *prefix*, masked, or -1.
  private def find(prefix : Prefix) : Int32
    node = prefix.v6? ? 1 : 0
    value = prefix.ip.to_u128
    top = prefix.bit_length - 1
    depth = 0
    while depth < prefix.length && node >= 0
      node = ((value >> (top - depth)) & 1_u128) == 0_u128 ? @zero[node] : @one[node]
      depth = depth + 1
    end
    node
  end

  # The length of the longest member holding *ip*, or -1.
  private def deepest(ip : IP) : Int32
    node = ip.v6? ? 1 : 0
    value = ip.to_u128
    bits = ip.bit_length
    best = -1
    depth = 0
    while node >= 0
      best = depth if @member[node]
      break if depth == bits
      node = ((value >> (bits - 1 - depth)) & 1_u128) == 0_u128 ? @zero[node] : @one[node]
      depth = depth + 1
    end
    best
  end

  # Members (or, given *full*, the highest full nodes) below *node*, in
  # order: a node before its children, the 0 side first.
  private def collect(node : Int32, depth : Int32, value : UInt128, v6 : ::Bool, result : Array(Prefix), full : Array(::Bool)?) : Nil
    bits = v6 ? 128 : 32
    if full
      if full[node]
        result << Prefix.new(NetipBits.ip(v6, value << (bits - depth)), depth)
        return
      end
    elsif @member[node]
      result << Prefix.new(NetipBits.ip(v6, value << (bits - depth)), depth)
    end
    zero = @zero[node]
    one = @one[node]
    collect(zero, depth + 1, value << 1, v6, result, full) if zero >= 0
    collect(one, depth + 1, (value << 1) | 1_u128, v6, result, full) if one >= 0
  end
end

# The reading and writing beneath the three types above.
struct NetipBits
  # Python 3.14's `_IPv4Constants._private_networks`: the blocks the IANA
  # IPv4 registry marks not globally reachable, and inside them the ones
  # that are (`_private_networks_exceptions`).
  NOT_GLOBAL_V4 = blocks([
    "0.0.0.0/8",          # "this network"
    "10.0.0.0/8",         # RFC 1918
    "127.0.0.0/8",        # loopback
    "169.254.0.0/16",     # link local
    "172.16.0.0/12",      # RFC 1918
    "192.0.0.0/24",       # IETF protocol assignments
    "192.0.0.170/31",     # NAT64/DNS64 discovery
    "192.0.2.0/24",       # TEST-NET-1
    "192.168.0.0/16",     # RFC 1918
    "198.18.0.0/15",      # benchmarking
    "198.51.100.0/24",    # TEST-NET-2
    "203.0.113.0/24",     # TEST-NET-3
    "240.0.0.0/4",        # reserved
    "255.255.255.255/32", # limited broadcast
  ])
  GLOBAL_V4 = blocks([
    "192.0.0.9/32",  # Port Control Protocol anycast
    "192.0.0.10/32", # TURN anycast
  ])

  # `_IPv6Constants._private_networks` and its exceptions.
  NOT_GLOBAL_V6 = blocks([
    "::1/128",        # loopback
    "::/128",         # unspecified
    "::ffff:0:0/96",  # IPv4-mapped
    "64:ff9b:1::/48", # local-use IPv4/IPv6 translation
    "100::/64",       # discard-only
    "2001::/23",      # IETF protocol assignments
    "2001:db8::/32",  # documentation
    "2002::/16",      # 6to4
    "3fff::/20",      # documentation (RFC 9637)
    "fc00::/7",       # unique local
    "fe80::/10",      # link local
  ])
  GLOBAL_V6 = blocks([
    "2001:1::1/128",   # Port Control Protocol anycast
    "2001:1::2/128",   # TURN anycast
    "2001:3::/32",     # AMT
    "2001:4:112::/48", # AS112-v6
    "2001:20::/28",    # ORCHIDv2
    "2001:30::/28",    # drone remote ID
  ])

  # `_IPv6Constants._reserved_networks`: RFC 4291's unassigned space.
  RESERVED_V6 = blocks([
    "::/8", "100::/8", "200::/7", "400::/6", "800::/5", "1000::/4", "4000::/3", "6000::/3",
    "8000::/3", "a000::/3", "c000::/3", "e000::/4", "f000::/5", "f800::/6", "fe00::/9",
  ])

  # The tables above as prefixes, read by the module's own reader: text
  # as the registries and Python write it, rather than bits by hand.
  def self.blocks(texts : Array(String)) : Array(Prefix)
    texts.map { |text| Prefix.parse(text).or_panic }
  end

  # Whether *value* is in the block *base*/*length* of a *bits*-bit family.
  def self.within(value : UInt128, base : UInt128, length : Int32, bits : Int32) : ::Bool
    (value >> (bits - length)) == (base >> (bits - length))
  end

  def self.listed(value : UInt128, blocks : Array(Prefix), bits : Int32) : ::Bool
    blocks.any? { |block| within(value, block.ip.to_u128, block.length, bits) }
  end

  def self.all_ones(v6 : ::Bool) : UInt128
    v6 ? ~0_u128 : 0xFFFFFFFF_u128
  end

  # The first *length* bits of the family set.
  def self.netmask(v6 : ::Bool, length : Int32) : UInt128
    all_ones(v6) & ~(all_ones(v6) >> length)
  end

  def self.ip(v6 : ::Bool, value : UInt128) : IP
    v6 ? IP.v6(value) : IP.v4(value.to_u32)
  end

  def self.check_length(ip : IP, length : Int32) : Nil
    if length < 0 || length > ip.bit_length
      raise "Prefix: /#{length} for #{ip.v6? ? "an IPv6" : "an IPv4"} address; its length is 0 to #{ip.bit_length}"
    end
  end

  # The byte offset of *byte* in *text* from *from*, or -1.
  def self.find(text : String, byte : UInt8, from : Int32) : Int32
    raw = text.to_unsafe
    index = from
    while index < text.bytesize
      return index if raw[index] == byte
      index = index + 1
    end
    -1
  end

  # A refusal's sentence without its `netip: ` head, to be part of another.
  def self.reason(message : String) : String
    message.starts_with?("netip: ") ? message[7, message.bytesize - 7] : message
  end

  # Why a zone is refused, or nil: Python's `_split_scope_id`.
  def self.zone_refused(zone : String) : String?
    return "the zone after '%' is empty" if zone.empty?
    return "the zone #{zone.inspect} holds a '%'" if find(zone, 37_u8, 0) >= 0
    nil
  end

  # Four octets, Python's `_parse_octet` for each: ASCII digits, one to
  # three of them, no leading zero, at most 255. The answer is the 32
  # bits, or why not.
  def self.read_v4(text : String) : UInt128 | String
    raw = text.to_unsafe
    size = text.bytesize
    return "an empty string is not an address" if size == 0
    value = 0_u128
    octets = 0
    at = 0
    while true
      start = at
      while at < size && raw[at] != 46_u8
        at = at + 1
      end
      octets = octets + 1
      return "more than four octets" if octets > 4
      count = at - start
      return "octet #{octets} is empty" if count == 0
      octet = text[start, count]
      number = 0
      index = start
      while index < at
        return "the octet #{octet.inspect} is not decimal digits" unless raw[index] >= 48_u8 && raw[index] <= 57_u8
        number = number * 10 + (raw[index] - 48_u8).to_i32 if index - start < 4
        index = index + 1
      end
      return "the octet #{octet.inspect} has more than three digits" if count > 3
      return "the octet #{octet.inspect} has a leading zero, which some readers take for octal" if count > 1 && raw[start] == 48_u8
      return "the octet #{octet} is past 255" if number > 255
      value = (value << 8) | number.to_u128
      break if at == size
      at = at + 1
    end
    return "#{octets} octets, not four" if octets < 4
    value
  end

  # RFC 4291 text, zone already taken off: Python's
  # `_BaseV6._ip_int_from_string`, step for step.
  def self.read_v6(text : String) : UInt128 | String
    return "the address before '%' is empty" if text.empty?
    return "#{text.bytesize} characters, past the 45 an IPv6 address is written in" if text.bytesize > 45
    parts = text.split(':')
    return "fewer than two colons" if parts.size < 3
    tail = parts[parts.size - 1]
    if find(tail, 46_u8, 0) >= 0
      four = read_v4(tail)
      return "the IPv4 tail #{tail.inspect}: #{four}" if four.is_a?(String)
      parts.pop
      parts << ((four >> 16) & 0xFFFF_u128).to_i32.to_s(16)
      parts << (four & 0xFFFF_u128).to_i32.to_s(16)
    end
    return "more than eight colons" if parts.size > 9
    skip = -1
    index = 1
    while index < parts.size - 1
      if parts[index].empty?
        return "more than one '::'" if skip >= 0
        skip = index
      end
      index = index + 1
    end
    high = 0
    low = 0
    skipped = 0
    if skip >= 0
      high = skip
      low = parts.size - skip - 1
      if parts[0].empty?
        high = high - 1
        return "a leading ':' that is not part of '::'" if high != 0
      end
      if parts[parts.size - 1].empty?
        low = low - 1
        return "a trailing ':' that is not part of '::'" if low != 0
      end
      skipped = 8 - high - low
      return "'::' beside eight groups stands for none" if skipped < 1
    else
      return "#{parts.size} groups and no '::', where eight are written without one" if parts.size != 8
      return "a leading ':' that is not part of '::'" if parts[0].empty?
      return "a trailing ':' that is not part of '::'" if parts[7].empty?
      high = 8
    end
    value = 0_u128
    index = 0
    while index < high
      group = read_hextet(parts[index])
      return group if group.is_a?(String)
      value = (value << 16) | group
      index = index + 1
    end
    value = value << (16 * skipped)
    index = parts.size - low
    while index < parts.size
      group = read_hextet(parts[index])
      return group if group.is_a?(String)
      value = (value << 16) | group
      index = index + 1
    end
    value
  end

  # One group: one to four hex digits.
  def self.read_hextet(text : String) : UInt128 | String
    return "an empty group" if text.empty?
    raw = text.to_unsafe
    value = 0_u128
    index = 0
    while index < text.bytesize
      byte = raw[index]
      digit = if byte >= 48_u8 && byte <= 57_u8
                byte - 48_u8
              elsif byte >= 97_u8 && byte <= 102_u8
                byte - 87_u8
              elsif byte >= 65_u8 && byte <= 70_u8
                byte - 55_u8
              else
                return "the group #{text.inspect} is not hex digits"
              end
      value = (value << 4) | digit.to_u128 if index < 4
      index = index + 1
    end
    return "the group #{text.inspect} has more than four digits" if text.bytesize > 4
    value
  end

  # A prefix's length after its '/': Python's `_make_netmask`. Decimal
  # digits alone (leading zeros allowed), at most the family's width; or,
  # for IPv4, a dotted netmask (ones, then zeros) or hostmask (zeros, then
  # ones), the two ambiguous masks read as netmasks. -1 when it is none.
  def self.read_length(text : String, v6 : ::Bool) : Int32
    bits = v6 ? 128 : 32
    raw = text.to_unsafe
    digits = text.bytesize > 0
    number = 0
    index = 0
    while index < text.bytesize
      if raw[index] >= 48_u8 && raw[index] <= 57_u8
        number = number * 10 + (raw[index] - 48_u8).to_i32 if number <= bits
      else
        digits = false
      end
      index = index + 1
    end
    return number <= bits ? number : -1 if digits
    return -1 if v6
    mask = read_v4(text)
    return -1 if mask.is_a?(String)
    length = contiguous(mask)
    return length if length >= 0
    contiguous(mask ^ 0xFFFFFFFF_u128)
  end

  # The length of a 32-bit netmask, or -1 when the ones are not first.
  private def self.contiguous(mask : UInt128) : Int32
    length = 0
    while length < 32 && ((mask >> (31 - length)) & 1_u128) == 1_u128
      length = length + 1
    end
    (mask & 0xFFFFFFFF_u128) == netmask(false, length) ? length : -1
  end

  def self.dotted(value : UInt128) : String
    "#{(value >> 24) & 255_u128}.#{(value >> 16) & 255_u128}.#{(value >> 8) & 255_u128}.#{value & 255_u128}"
  end

  # Group *index*, 0 to 7, of a 128-bit value.
  def self.hextet(value : UInt128, index : Int32) : Int32
    ((value >> (112 - 16 * index)) & 0xFFFF_u128).to_i32
  end

  # RFC 5952 section 4.2: the longest run of two or more zero groups as
  # `::`, the first of equal runs.
  def self.hextets(value : UInt128) : String
    best = -1
    best_size = 1
    start = -1
    index = 0
    while index < 8
      if hextet(value, index) == 0
        start = index if start < 0
        if index - start + 1 > best_size
          best = start
          best_size = index - start + 1
        end
      else
        start = -1
      end
      index = index + 1
    end
    String.build do |io|
      index = 0
      while index < 8
        if index == best
          io << "::"
          index = index + best_size
          next
        end
        io << ':' if index > 0 && index != best + best_size
        io << hextet(value, index).to_s(16)
        index = index + 1
      end
    end
  end
end
